  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 243 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe upper level high will weaken a bit and shift somewhat to the \nsoutheast today.  As a result, this will allow for monsoon moisture \nto move into most of the area with weak flow aloft.  Meanwhile, a \ncool front was moving across nern CO early this morning with \neasterly low level flow behind it. As a result, it won't be as hot\nacross the plains as readings this aftn will range from the upper\n80's to mid 90's.\n\nAs fas as precip, will see sct slow moving showers and storms this \naftn over the higher terrain.  PW values will be 150% to 175% above \nnormal over the higher terrain so expect some heavier rainfall in \nsome areas.  As far as the plains, outflow boundaries from tstms \nover the higher terrain will move across the I-25 Corridor by late \naftn or early evening.  Models disagree on where main focus will be \nas HRRR and GFS favor Fort Collins northward while ECMWF is further \nsouth over the Denver metro.  Overall, expect aditional tstm \nactivity to develop by late aftn or early evening from Denver north \nto the WY border. With increasing ESE low level flow this evening\nalong with PW's above 150%, may see heavier rainfall across nrn \nportions of the I-25 Corridor. Meanwhile, depending on how far \noutflow boundaries move across the plains, additional showers and \nstorms may linger overnight over portions of the nern plains. \n\nFor Wed, will continue to see monsoon moisture moving across the \narea as the main upper level high is centered to the southeast of \nthe area.  PW's across the high terrain will be from 150% to 200% \nabove normal so expect areas of heavier rainfall during the aftn \nwith the stronger storms.  Across the plains, the low level flow \nwill become southeast so expect a Denver cyclone to develop by aftn.\nShould see tstms develop along the associated convergence zone with \nstorms move slowly to the east from late aftn thru the evening \nhours.  Additional storms may also develop along outflow boundaries \nmoving across from the higher terrain as well.  Once again with PW's \naoa 150% or normal expect areas of heavy rainfall. Highs on Wed, \nwill remain in the upper 80's to mid 90's over nern CO.\n\nBy Thu, monsoon moisture will continue over the area as a weak upper \nlevel trough moves across the area. PW's are still fcst to be \nfrom 150% to 200% above normal. Thus should see another round of \nshowers and storms across much of the area with areas of heavy \nrainfall. Highs on Thu will be in the upper 80's to lower 90's \nacross the plains.\n\nLooking ahead to Fri and Sat, the upper level high will gradually \nbuild back into the area as monsoon moisture decreases.  As a \nresult, will see tstm activity more sct over the higher terrain with \nonly a slight chc of storms over the plains both days. Highs will be \nwarmer as well as readings rise back into the mid 90's to lower \n100's across the plains by Sat.\n\nFor Sun, an upper level high will extend from the Great Basin across \nColorado.  There are substantial differences between the ECMWF and \nGFS with regards to whether there will be another surge of \nmonsoon moisture. The ECMWF shows another surge while the GFS \ndoesn't so it's not clear how much tstm activity there will be. \nMeanwhile temperatures will remain in the mid 90's to lower 100's \nfor highs across nern CO.",
